00:18:44,281 --> 00:18:50,280
and even if it's unintentional, if they go in, you know, the tiger gets your fingers, you won't come back out with it.

226
00:18:50,281 --> 00:18:57,820
Every detail of the loading process has been worked out by the Fort Worth Zoo and DHL before even arriving at the airport.

227
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:10,080
The crate is moved onto the plane by a K-loader, which is an elevator device used to move cargo up into the plane's hold.

228
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:14,780
We're going to be loading the Tiger in what we call position three on the aircraft.

229
00:19:14,781 --> 00:19:21,080
This allows us to put the Tiger on last on the plane and also allows us to remove it from the aircraft first.

230
00:19:21,340 --> 00:19:26,260
We've briefed the flight crew in regards to the temperature that's needed to keep the animal calm.

231
00:19:26,680 --> 00:19:30,500
We're going to keep this particular aircraft at a 60 to 70 degree temperature,

232
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:34,120
which will allow the animal to remain at its normal temperature.

233
00:19:34,121 --> 00:19:45,220
The trip to Miami takes four hours.

234
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:48,100
It includes one brief stopover in Cincinnati.

235
00:19:51,580 --> 00:19:56,880
The Tigers' plane is greeted in the Sunshine State by waiting members of the Miami Metro Zoo,

236
00:19:57,660 --> 00:20:00,340
the press, and DHL attendants.

237
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:18,120
Almost all of the zoos have the same practice with incoming animals, and we usually put

238
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:19,700
them through a 30-day quarantine.

239
00:20:19,701 --> 00:20:25,240
The crate will be butted up against and secured so that it can't slip back.

240
00:20:25,241 --> 00:20:29,840
At that time, then the locks are removed from the crate, and the two doors will be opened,

241
00:20:29,841 --> 00:20:33,580
then the cat has the opportunity to leave the shipping container.

242
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:40,460
A very important aspect of this move that we did here today,

243
00:20:40,660 --> 00:20:41,900
sending Hathi to Miami,

244
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:44,680
is the fact that these tigers will reproduce,

245
00:20:44,940 --> 00:20:46,080
that we are cooperating,

246
00:20:46,081 --> 00:20:49,920
and we're going to have tigers for many, many years

247
00:20:49,921 --> 00:20:52,540
for our children and our children's children to see

248
00:20:52,541 --> 00:20:55,300
at zoos here in the United States.

249
00:20:55,980 --> 00:20:58,160
Tigers aren't the only dangerous cargo

250
00:20:58,161 --> 00:20:59,960
that need quick transport by jet,

251
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:02,740
life-saving radioactive pharmaceuticals

252
00:21:02,741 --> 00:21:05,440
are often rushed to hospitals in Learjets.

253
00:21:07,380 --> 00:21:10,440
Because of the Species Survival Plan breeding program,

254
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,200
the captive Indochinese tiger population

255
00:21:13,201 --> 00:21:14,820
has seen a dramatic increase,

256
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:17,760
growing from only 10 tigers in 1998

257
00:21:17,761 --> 00:21:20,860
to 44 in 2004.

258
00:21:22,040 --> 00:21:23,920
More dangerous cargo will return

259
00:21:23,921 --> 00:21:25,520
on Modern Marvels.

260
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:33,160
Handling radioactive materials is always dangerous.

261
00:21:33,161 --> 00:21:39,160
Even more so when doing it quickly is part of the equation.

262
00:21:39,161 --> 00:21:44,160
Radioactive pharmaceuticals have become important tools in modern medicine.

263
00:21:44,161 --> 00:21:50,160
After being injected into the body, they allow physicians to observe the cells at work.

264
00:21:50,161 --> 00:21:54,160
They are especially useful as an advanced form of cancer detection.

265
00:21:54,161 --> 00:21:56,920
However, there is a catch to their effectiveness.

266
00:21:58,340 --> 00:22:02,360
Some of the drugs have a potency lifespan measured only in hours.

267
00:22:03,540 --> 00:22:09,160
One of the very short-lived radioactive pharmaceuticals is fluorodeoxyglucose.

268
00:22:09,340 --> 00:22:12,260
This material has a half-life of 110 minutes,

269
00:22:12,820 --> 00:22:18,040
which means approximately every two hours, 50% of the material decays by normal processes.

270
00:22:18,860 --> 00:22:22,880
In order to rush the drug to hospitals and pharmacies in distant cities,

271
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:29,220
drug manufacturers like Cardinal Health have turned to air carriers to transport them at high speeds.

272
00:22:32,980 --> 00:22:37,240
The success of the air carrier in delivering this radioactive material on time

273
00:22:37,241 --> 00:22:42,340
can literally be a matter of life and death to a patient waiting in some faraway city.

274
00:22:43,460 --> 00:22:46,700
Yet their transport must always be done with extreme caution.

275
00:22:46,701 --> 00:22:57,200
Here, Cardinal Health is creating a new shipment of the short-lived fluorodeoxyglucose, which is also known as FDG.

276
00:22:59,020 --> 00:23:05,360
A crucial moment in the process is when the drug is bombarded with radioactive protons in the lab's cyclotron.

277
00:23:09,160 --> 00:23:15,200
During the bombardment process, a significant amount of radiation is generated by the proton bombardment.

278
00:23:15,201 --> 00:23:15,901
lombard.

279
00:23:15,902 --> 00:23:21,140
Therefore, we make sure that the vault is sealed and personnel entry is forbidden.

280
00:23:21,640 --> 00:23:27,340
All of our workers wear radiation monitoring devices to ensure that we monitor at all times

281
00:23:27,341 --> 00:23:29,432
exposures and exposure histories.

282
00:23:29,433 --> 00:23:33,300
From this moment on, the drug is synthesized into a hazardous

283
00:23:33,301 --> 00:23:36,915
material, or hazmat, and is treated accordingly.

284
00:23:36,916 --> 00:23:40,380
Following the synthesis of FDG, it's delivered

285
00:23:40,381 --> 00:23:44,020
directly into a lead-lined cabinet, which is called a hot cell.

286
00:23:44,260 --> 00:23:47,160
The walls of the lead cabinet are approximately three inches thick

287
00:23:47,161 --> 00:23:51,360
as necessary to safely shield personnel during the handling of this material.

288
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:57,520
We use remote manipulators, devices that allow the handling of the product

289
00:23:57,521 --> 00:23:59,180
from outside the hot cell.

290
00:24:00,620 --> 00:24:04,140
Cardinal Health depends on techniques developed over the last half century

291
00:24:04,141 --> 00:24:05,960
by the radiopharmaceutical industry.

292
00:24:05,961 --> 00:24:12,580
This industry is a direct byproduct of America's experiments with nuclear energy during World War II.

293
00:24:16,960 --> 00:24:21,960
Radioactive materials in medicine for all practical purposes began in 1946

294
00:24:21,961 --> 00:24:25,620
as an outshoot of the Manhattan Project in Oak Ridge, Tennessee.

295
00:24:26,340 --> 00:24:32,360
At that time, radioactive materials were distributed for research purposes to a number of hospitals in the U.S.

296
00:24:32,361 --> 00:24:38,500
Due to the short-lived potency of the drugs, planes became the preferred mode for shipping them.

297
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:46,840
However, regulations involving how the drugs were packaged and transported were sometimes violated.

298
00:24:47,540 --> 00:24:52,140
Several incidents involving radioactive leakage spurred a call for tighter regulations.

299
00:24:53,420 --> 00:24:58,620
During the 1970s, a variety of situations arose where there was leakage

300
00:24:58,621 --> 00:25:03,200
or problems that occurred with packages that didn't meet sufficient standards

301
00:25:03,201 --> 00:25:05,320
to contain the radioactive elements.

302
00:25:06,020 --> 00:25:09,960
The Transportation Safety Act of 1974 addressed these concerns,

303
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:13,820
and the packaging and shipping of the drugs became more tightly regulated.

304
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:21,080
Today, radioactive pharmaceuticals are transported in what are called type A containers,

305
00:25:21,580 --> 00:25:23,260
which are made of lead or tungsten.

306
00:25:24,280 --> 00:25:28,280
The type A container is tested pursuant to government requirements.

307
00:25:28,620 --> 00:25:33,500
to ensure the integrity of the product in drop tests, puncture resistance,

308
00:25:33,980 --> 00:25:39,660
and other forms of damage that might routinely be expected to incur during shipment.

309
00:25:40,380 --> 00:25:45,660
After being sealed, the container is checked for any radioactive leakage and labeled.

310
00:25:50,700 --> 00:25:54,760
Next, it's placed in a special metal box called an ammo can.

311
00:25:54,761 --> 00:26:02,740
It's basically a container that's used by military, in prior use that is, to contain ammunition.

312
00:26:02,741 --> 00:26:07,080
Because these containers stand up well to the rigors of transportation,

313
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:12,720
they've also been applied to the manner in which radioactive materials are often shipped today.

314
00:26:13,760 --> 00:26:17,000
The canisters are given one last radiation check.

315
00:26:19,340 --> 00:26:22,160
Just ten minutes after coming out of the hot cell,

316
00:26:22,161 --> 00:26:26,160
The pharmaceuticals are handed over to a driver for AirNet,

317
00:26:26,161 --> 00:26:29,160
a Columbus, Ohio-based air express company.

318
00:26:33,160 --> 00:26:37,160
Then it's off to the airport, ten miles away.

319
00:26:37,161 --> 00:26:42,160
AirNet operates a fleet of Lear jets to speed their cargoes as quickly as possible.

320
00:26:42,161 --> 00:26:45,160
Our system is built for speed.

321
00:26:45,161 --> 00:26:48,160
The backbone of our national air system is the Lear 35.

322
00:26:48,161 --> 00:26:50,160
It's a very dependable, fast aircraft.

323
00:26:50,161 --> 00:26:54,160
It's perfectly suited for the customer base that we serve.

324
00:26:54,161 --> 00:26:59,160
Although traditional air carriers also carry radio pharmaceuticals,

325
00:26:59,161 --> 00:27:04,160
AirNet has found its niche in the air cargo business through specialization in the field.

326
00:27:04,161 --> 00:27:08,160
The company handles quite a bit of dangerous goods, hazardous material,

327
00:27:08,161 --> 00:27:15,160
including the transportation of radioactive product, medicine, radio pharmaceuticals.

328
00:27:15,161 --> 00:27:21,820
Because of its specially trained staff, AirNet can handle larger quantities of radioactive goods than traditional carriers.

329
00:27:24,100 --> 00:27:29,860
We set out on a nine-month process to prepare our company with function-specific training

330
00:27:29,861 --> 00:27:36,960
to allow our handlers, pilots, loaders, the experience to be experts in this industry.

331
00:27:37,480 --> 00:27:41,840
And we carry more time-critical radiopharmaceuticals than anyone else in the industry.

332
00:27:42,640 --> 00:27:48,280
Once at the AirNet facility, the drugs are sent through a check-in procedure designed for efficiency and speed.

333
00:27:48,980 --> 00:27:58,600
The shipment is reviewed to ensure that the packaging is in good condition, that all documentation has been completed.

334
00:28:03,060 --> 00:28:04,300
Next, they're weighed.

335
00:28:05,400 --> 00:28:07,140
Finally, they're rushed to the plane.

336
00:28:07,141 --> 00:28:13,380
The sense of urgency is so great during the process that Air Net employees often run through their duties.

337
00:28:14,100 --> 00:28:21,360
During our process, we expect all components to be done expeditiously and safely.

338
00:28:22,160 --> 00:28:25,080
On the plane, the drugs are secured in the cargo hold.

339
00:28:26,020 --> 00:28:34,440
The radioactive level material on the aircraft is carefully monitored before takeoff to ensure that there are no issues.

340
00:28:35,300 --> 00:28:38,320
With all in order, it's time for an immediate takeoff.

341
00:28:38,980 --> 00:28:41,540
It's been just 30 minutes since the drugs were manufactured.

342
00:28:45,400 --> 00:28:47,620
Flights rarely last more than two hours

343
00:28:47,621 --> 00:28:51,500
because of the urgency of getting the pharmaceuticals to the hospitals on time.

344
00:28:52,540 --> 00:28:54,780
Think of it as a melting ice cube.

345
00:28:55,900 --> 00:28:57,880
From its base in Columbus, Ohio,

346
00:28:58,340 --> 00:29:01,900
and with a Learjet speed of 440 nautical miles per hour,

347
00:29:02,700 --> 00:29:07,940
AirNet can easily deliver the drug to cities such as Chicago and New York within the two-hour time frame.

348
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:14,900
So a patient is waiting on the other end of our shipment to know whether they have cancer

349
00:29:14,901 --> 00:29:19,060
or whether it's some other indication that the doctor is looking for.

350
00:29:19,061 --> 00:29:25,020
We must move expeditiously from the time we pick up the package to the time we tender it to our aircraft,

351
00:29:25,520 --> 00:29:27,800
to the time that aircraft lands, to the time it's delivered.

352
00:29:27,801 --> 00:29:32,180
Now it's just a short trip for the drugs to the hospital.

353
00:29:33,180 --> 00:29:37,800
The delivery of this cargo on time is much more than just a delivery deadline met.

354
00:29:38,360 --> 00:29:42,140
It's an important mission accomplished in trying to prolong someone's life.

355
00:29:43,040 --> 00:29:49,180
The job of hauling dangerous cargo is full of peril, perhaps no more so than in times of war.

356
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:56,680
During World War II, the merchant marine had to transport munitions through enemy submarine-infested waters.

357
00:29:57,800 --> 00:30:08,980
In the United States, over 14 million doses of radioactive pharmaceuticals are used annually in the diagnosis of disease.

358
00:30:09,860 --> 00:30:12,900
More dangerous cargo will return on Modern Marvels.

359
00:30:12,901 --> 00:30:26,901
Among the greatest unsung heroes of World War II

360
00:30:28,120 --> 00:30:31,580
were the officers and crew members of the Merchant Marine.

361
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:37,060
Without their heroic efforts, the war may have been lost or prolonged by years.

362
00:30:37,061 --> 00:30:46,060
These were the men who transported the bombs, the fuel, the dangerous cargo needed by the Allies to defeat the Axis enemy.

363
00:30:47,120 --> 00:30:53,520
Without the supplies, without the tanks and the guns and the ammunition and the food, there would be no armies.

364
00:30:53,720 --> 00:31:00,580
Because the armies could not survive unless the Merchant Marine carried these goods across the oceans to them.

365
00:31:01,140 --> 00:31:06,240
The Merchant Marine is a fleet of ships which carries imports and exports during peacetime.

366
00:31:07,060 --> 00:31:10,480
but becomes a naval auxiliary during wartime to deliver cargo.

367
00:31:12,540 --> 00:31:16,040
The men who served on the ships weren't members of the armed forces.

368
00:31:17,240 --> 00:31:18,140
They are all civilians.

369
00:31:18,600 --> 00:31:22,800
They joined on their own accord and joined the merchant marine,

370
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:25,660
which is, you had to go through the Coast Guard to get your seaman's papers.

371
00:31:26,140 --> 00:31:27,820
He became a merchant seaman.

372
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:32,100
Although not in uniform, the crew members of the merchant ships

373
00:31:32,101 --> 00:31:34,020
not only transported dangerous cargo,

374
00:31:34,580 --> 00:31:38,340
They often faced battle situations that equaled those of anyone in the service.

375
00:31:41,680 --> 00:31:46,640
A torpedo is built to sink just about anything it hits.

376
00:31:46,820 --> 00:31:50,040
And when you got hit by a torpedo, depending on where it hit the ship,

377
00:31:50,540 --> 00:31:51,940
you know, that was a death knell.

378
00:31:52,640 --> 00:31:54,760
If it was in the wintertime in the North Atlantic,

379
00:31:54,761 --> 00:31:58,300
if you got off the ship, you're probably going to freeze to death.

380
00:31:58,460 --> 00:32:02,400
If it was a tanker, now you're on a fireball, you know.

381
00:32:02,401 --> 00:32:05,360
It was very, very horrendous.

382
00:32:09,980 --> 00:32:13,600
The loss of life on these vessels was truly staggering.

383
00:32:13,980 --> 00:32:21,620
It was dangerous enough that the merchant marine during World War II, percentage-wise, lost more people,

384
00:32:22,060 --> 00:32:24,780
and more people killed than any of the armed services.

385
00:32:24,781 --> 00:32:31,780
During the early years of the war, German submarines sank Allied merchant ships as fast as they could be built.

386
00:32:31,781 --> 00:32:38,780
Realizing the war could be lost if the supply lines between the United States and Europe were cut,

387
00:32:38,781 --> 00:32:42,780
the Allies began to build Liberty ships.

388
00:32:42,781 --> 00:32:47,780
The whole idea was to build a reliable ship, extraordinarily fast.

389
00:32:47,781 --> 00:32:52,780
The Liberty ships were 411 feet long and 62 feet wide.

390
00:32:52,781 --> 00:32:53,481
feet wide.

391
00:32:53,482 --> 00:32:58,300
They were mass produced in assembly line fashion, with each ship having interchangeable

392
00:32:58,301 --> 00:32:59,001
parts.

393
00:32:59,002 --> 00:33:07,300
The first ship took 244 days to construct, but by May of 1942, that number was cut to

394
00:33:07,301 --> 00:33:12,408
72, and by August of 42, they were being assembled in 46 days.

395
00:33:12,409 --> 00:33:14,220
Although Allied fleets

396
00:33:14,221 --> 00:33:18,580
were now growing in number, the ships were still extremely susceptible to German U-boat

397
00:33:18,581 --> 00:33:19,439
attacks.

398
00:33:19,440 --> 00:33:28,240
Many of the first Liberty ships were unarmed, making them easy pickings for German

399
00:33:28,241 --> 00:33:31,517
submarines who didn't even use torpedoes to sink them.

400
00:33:31,518 --> 00:33:33,520
If the ships couldn't fight back,

401
00:33:33,580 --> 00:33:38,660
you could surface and use your deck gun and you could sink a lot of ships on the surface,

402
00:33:38,880 --> 00:33:41,668
not wasting a torpedo on a merchant ship.

403
00:33:41,669 --> 00:33:45,000
It was decided to protect the cargo carriers with

404
00:33:45,001 --> 00:33:46,197
armed Navy guards.

405
00:33:46,198 --> 00:33:51,320
But even with his added firepower, the ships were still extremely vulnerable.

406
00:33:52,840 --> 00:33:54,113
We were called the sitting ducks.

407
00:33:54,114 --> 00:33:56,080
They'd say to somebody, you know, I'm God, I'd say,

408
00:33:56,140 --> 00:33:58,402
I'm God, what are you, mad? You're crazy?

409
00:33:58,403 --> 00:34:01,160
Because, you know, the ships were minimally armed.

410
00:34:05,300 --> 00:34:06,214
It was no fun.

411
00:34:06,215 --> 00:34:11,440
Although the guards were effective enough to stop German submarines from picking

412
00:34:11,441 --> 00:34:15,840
off Liberty ships with their deck guns. They couldn't stop the torpedo attacks.

413
00:34:16,700 --> 00:34:21,140
It was scary when in the middle of the night everything is blacked out, you can't see anything

414
00:34:21,141 --> 00:34:22,942
and then you see this explosion.

415
00:34:22,943 --> 00:34:26,660
Perhaps the most perilous part of their transatlantic journey took

416
00:34:26,661 --> 00:34:32,380
place in the air gap, a wide band of the North Atlantic that was not patrolled by Allied aircraft

417
00:34:32,381 --> 00:34:34,863
and took four to five days to cross.

418
00:34:34,864 --> 00:34:39,000
You lost your air cover and this is when you were out in the

419
00:34:39,001 --> 00:34:41,704
middle of the ocean, and this is what the Germans knew.

420
00:34:41,705 --> 00:34:43,720
They didn't want to be operating close to

421
00:34:43,721 --> 00:34:47,980
the shores where they could be discovered by the airplane, so you were on your own.

422
00:34:48,620 --> 00:34:54,240
If stricken at sea and unable to move on with a convoy, ships were often left behind.

423
00:34:54,880 --> 00:34:59,660
The ship could not keep up with the convoy, and they had engine problems,

424
00:34:59,661 --> 00:35:05,520
because the convoy could not stop for them. The convoy kept moving all of the time.

425
00:35:05,521 --> 00:35:12,520
In an effort to bolster the speed of the merchant marine fleet, victory ships were introduced in 1944.

426
00:35:12,521 --> 00:35:23,520
The victory ships could reach a maximum speed of 17 knots, a 7 knot increase over the Liberty ships, giving them a better chance of avoiding Nazi subs.

427
00:35:23,521 --> 00:35:30,520
ultimately the liberty and victory ships delivered the goods

428
00:35:30,521 --> 00:35:34,760
by wars and carried over seventy five percent of the supplies needed to assure

429
00:35:34,761 --> 00:35:39,760
victory for the allies

430
00:35:39,761 --> 00:35:44,680
who served on liberty and victory ships and the ultimate dangerous cargo jobs

431
00:35:44,681 --> 00:35:49,680
and they paid the ultimate price

432
00:35:49,681 --> 00:35:56,680
In World War II, nearly 6,000 men perished while serving in the American Merchant Marine.

433
00:36:02,680 --> 00:36:09,680
A total of 2,751 Liberty and 531 Victory ships were built during World War II.

434
00:36:10,680 --> 00:36:14,680
More dangerous cargo will return on Modern Marvels.

435
00:36:14,681 --> 00:36:17,553
Hazardous materials, or hazmats.

436
00:36:17,554 --> 00:36:23,120
Explosive, toxic, radioactive, noxious, and just plain deadly.

437
00:36:24,420 --> 00:36:27,320
No one wants them, but we can't live without them.

438
00:36:28,860 --> 00:36:31,900
Hazmats are the nasty byproducts of our industrial age.

439
00:36:32,600 --> 00:36:35,140
They're the dirty stuff that makes the world go round.

440
00:36:37,300 --> 00:36:41,420
Our coming to grips with how to handle them has been a long and painful process.

441
00:36:44,680 --> 00:36:51,700
During the 19th and 20th centuries, hazardous materials were often just dumped into rivers and waterways and forgotten.

442
00:36:52,520 --> 00:36:56,280
Many took an out-of-sight, out-of-mind attitude when dealing with the substances.

443
00:36:56,840 --> 00:37:00,500
But the government did slowly move forward in trying to regulate them.

444
00:37:01,180 --> 00:37:11,840
There's been loss of life, loss of property, and loss of products that has caused significant regulation, compliance restrictions, and legislation.

445
00:37:11,841 --> 00:37:17,420
During the 1980s, federal legislation dealing with hazmats leaped forward.

446
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:23,240
As is often the case, it took a catastrophic disaster to spur positive legislation.

447
00:37:24,040 --> 00:37:26,540
The incident occurred in December of 1984.

448
00:37:28,600 --> 00:37:35,960
The most significant incident occurred when Union Carbide was operating a plant in Bhopal, India.

449
00:37:35,961 --> 00:37:42,120
and over the course of the late hours of the evening dispersed a chemical gas

450
00:37:42,121 --> 00:37:48,440
that early in the morning was found out that there was at least 2,500 people dead in their beds in their homes.

451
00:37:48,800 --> 00:37:53,640
A disaster immediately raised a call for stronger hazmat legislation in America.

452
00:37:54,140 --> 00:38:00,960
The laws were basically called the emergency planning law and the community right to know law.

453
00:38:00,961 --> 00:38:08,420
And what these processes did was instill in everybody that manufactured, handled, and processed chemicals

454
00:38:08,421 --> 00:38:14,300
that they now needed to report the chemicals on site in their facilities

455
00:38:14,301 --> 00:38:20,860
so that not only emergency response people could act immediately to any disruption due to a chemical spill,

456
00:38:21,220 --> 00:38:26,840
but also that the community had the right to know of what was transpiring within the four walls of these businesses.

457
00:38:27,180 --> 00:38:30,360
Stricter legislation drove many out of the hazmat business.

458
00:38:30,960 --> 00:38:35,720
But it was seen as an opportunity for others, such as the Weber Distribution Company,

459
00:38:36,140 --> 00:38:40,180
which specializes in transporting, warehousing, and distributing hazmats.

460
00:38:42,360 --> 00:38:48,600
Its state-of-the-art facility in Santa Fe Springs, California, serves as a storage terminal for hazmats in transit.

461
00:38:49,420 --> 00:38:55,300
The vast complex is divided into separate areas, each tailored to store specific hazardous materials.

462
00:38:55,301 --> 00:39:01,140
federal legislation forbids the commingling of hazmats that aren't compatible

463
00:39:01,141 --> 00:39:08,940
there are very strict requirements in regards to commingling and and compatibility with various

464
00:39:08,941 --> 00:39:14,620
chemicals that's why our particular building here in santa fe springs is sectioned off into

465
00:39:14,621 --> 00:39:21,180
14 different rooms one of the most carefully designed rooms is the one where poisons and

466
00:39:21,181 --> 00:39:22,586
toxins are stored.

467
00:39:22,587 --> 00:39:28,600
Today our poison room basically has cyanide materials in there, various types

468
00:39:28,601 --> 00:39:31,942
of cyanide, sodium, copper, potassium cyanide.

469
00:39:31,943 --> 00:39:34,920
Many safeguards, including the fire door,

470
00:39:35,200 --> 00:39:39,217
are built into the room in case some disaster should strike.

471
00:39:39,218 --> 00:39:41,560
The door is designed and engineered

472
00:39:41,561 --> 00:39:49,635
with a fusible link that would automatically close once it hits certain heat levels.

473
00:39:49,636 --> 00:39:50,336
This

474
00:39:50,337 --> 00:39:56,400
door would in fact guillotine down to help circumvent or mitigate any problems we had

475
00:39:56,401 --> 00:39:57,360
in the building.

476
00:39:57,361 --> 00:40:01,500
The room also features a special air filter system to protect against

477
00:40:01,501 --> 00:40:04,541
the emission of toxic fumes in case of an emergency.

478
00:40:04,542 --> 00:40:06,880
We have a charcoal filtered air scrubber

479
00:40:06,881 --> 00:40:12,927
that sucks up air throughout the building, funnels it through the system itself.

480
00:40:12,928 --> 00:40:13,628
In the

481
00:40:13,629 --> 00:40:20,380
event of fire, it would filter any fumes or contaminants before it enters the environment.

482
00:40:22,860 --> 00:40:25,410
The entire complex is computer controlled.

483
00:40:25,411 --> 00:40:27,960
With a click of a button, Weber can locate

484
00:40:27,961 --> 00:40:30,448
every object stored in the massive facility.

485
00:40:30,449 --> 00:40:32,880
Not only is this advantageous for inventory

486
00:40:32,881 --> 00:40:39,460
control, it's mandatory for safety. If the wrong product is pulled, it could cause

487
00:40:39,461 --> 00:40:45,620
severe damage in the manufacturing process or when loaded on the trucks with uncompatible product.

488
00:40:46,460 --> 00:40:53,940
We're very strict and detailed in identifying what product goes in what hazard classifications.

489
00:40:54,980 --> 00:40:59,820
Products can be stored in the warehouse for as little as a few hours or up to several years.

490
00:41:00,600 --> 00:41:03,200
The average time containers are kept here is three months.

491
00:41:05,260 --> 00:41:09,280
The room containing flammables has some of the most stringent safety measures.

492
00:41:09,460 --> 00:41:13,460
Even the forklifts that are used here are special.

493
00:41:13,461 --> 00:41:18,460
What we had to do was go out and bring in double E-rated forklifts.

494
00:41:18,461 --> 00:41:25,460
All of its operating mechanism, all of the electrical wiring, all the circuitry is in a steel box.

495
00:41:25,461 --> 00:41:32,460
And by putting them in steel boxes, you're lessening the issue and the problems of the emission of a spark.

496
00:41:32,461 --> 00:41:39,660
By eliminating the ignition source, we're cutting back on the possibility of a fire or an explosion.

497
00:41:40,320 --> 00:41:42,860
The room also features explosion-proof lighting.

498
00:41:43,540 --> 00:41:47,340
Explosion-proof lighting are simply lights that are hermetically sealed.

499
00:41:47,341 --> 00:41:55,680
Again, just the same as our EE-rated forklifts, all the electrical mechanisms are encased in a metal box

500
00:41:55,681 --> 00:42:00,560
so that if there's a spark, it won't get out into the environment, into the atmosphere of that room.

501
00:42:00,561 --> 00:42:04,440
The entire building has an elaborate fire sprinkler safety system.

502
00:42:05,100 --> 00:42:08,300
But in the flammable section, this precaution is taken even further

503
00:42:08,301 --> 00:42:11,380
by placing the sprinklers directly on the storage racks.

504
00:42:13,100 --> 00:42:15,980
There's a little fusible link inside the sprinkler heads,

505
00:42:15,981 --> 00:42:20,860
and once a certain temperature is accumulated, they will go off.

506
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:25,080
Now, normally that temperature is over 200 degrees.

507
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:29,140
Security at the chemical storage facility is a top priority.

508
00:42:30,560 --> 00:42:39,240
Security is very tight here, and it has gotten more strict since 9-11.

509
00:42:39,241 --> 00:42:43,900
Weber goes through a significant background check with all of our employees.

510
00:42:44,520 --> 00:42:55,240
This includes past employment history, Social Security validation, criminal background check, drug and alcohol testing.

511
00:42:56,320 --> 00:42:59,940
The tightest security on the grounds is for the area known as the cage.

512
00:43:00,560 --> 00:43:13,180
The chemicals that are stored in this particular area are precursors or could be used in such a form and fashion as an item to manufacture illicit drugs.

513
00:43:13,760 --> 00:43:17,320
These chemicals can also be used for the manufacture of legitimate drugs.

514
00:43:18,260 --> 00:43:22,260
Pharmaceutical companies turn to Weber to store them when they are not needed for production.

515
00:43:22,261 --> 00:43:32,300
Closed circuit surveillance cameras are trained on the area 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.

516
00:43:32,900 --> 00:43:45,320
There are only three individuals in our company and all supervisors or higher that are allowed to open and close the locked area for that product.

517
00:43:46,020 --> 00:43:51,920
The product is maintained in that room until it's escorted to put on the outbound vehicle.

518
00:43:52,260 --> 00:44:00,460
Like all other handlers of dangerous cargo, Weber has found you can never be too cautious or too prepared.

519
00:44:00,740 --> 00:44:11,260
If your employees are not properly trained, and if they're not aware of the commodities that are being stored in your building, it could be a recipe for disaster.

520
00:44:12,040 --> 00:44:17,240
The placards say it all. Transporting dangerous cargo is serious business.

521
00:44:17,900 --> 00:44:22,160
There are no easy routes, no shortcuts to the final destinations in this business.

522
00:44:22,260 --> 00:44:29,060
Those who deliver the goods on time are those who clearly see all the curves in the bumpy road they travel.

